  "notes": "National Airlines' LAX flights (a few a month) are 777Fs (N792CA seen as NCR645, adsb.lol 2026-11-20) and 747-400Fs; Commons has no photo of a National 777F, so this is drawn on the 747-400F (N537CA) and its placement on a 777F is a carry-over, not measured. Two versions of the scheme fly on the 747s: this one (the broad blue swoosh from the belly, the red line atop the fin, the red-and-blue logo, blue engines) on the 747-400F N537CA and 747-400ERF N663CA (2026), and N952CA (2022); the converted 747-400BCF N729CA wears a version with two thin blue lines in place of the swoosh, no red on the fin, no logo and white engines (liveries/NCR/thin-lines.json, tagged in airframes/NCR.json). Which the other BCFs (N702CA, N756CA, N919CA, N952CA) wear now isn't settled by 2024-2026 photos. N936CA wears a red and gold 30th-anniversary scheme (2022-2024 photos), not drawn. Measured on the right side (N537CA, 2026-03-18, fitted): the title runs x 0.090-0.379 with its capitals about 2.7 m tall, the block aft of it at 0.37-0.44, the logo at 0.457; the swoosh leaves the belly at x 0.60-0.65 and reaches the crown at 0.83-0.88. The left side (N537CA, 2023-11-15, a pitched take-off, read along the fuselage, not fitted): the block ahead at 0.07-0.14, the title 0.12-0.40, the logo at about the same place as on the right. The winglets are blue with thin white lines on their outer faces (lines not drawn); their inner faces aren't seen: drawn blue. The swoosh's continuation under the belly isn't seen. A small ® after the title is not drawn. Colours by eye.",
  "history": "New in the LAX round (2026-11-20); National had no scheme here before."
